{"context":"The architectural foundation of Anvil has been established across ten Architecture Decision Records. The architecture defines a framework-agnostic, portable, and extensible Runtime and Release Orchestration Toolkit. The next step is selecting an implementation language that can realize this architecture effectively.\n\nThe architecture and product requirements impose the following implementation needs:\n\n**Cross-platform execution.** Anvil must run consistently on Linux and macOS (PRD-001 §10). Developers use macOS during development. CI runners and production servers use Linux. A single codebase must serve both platforms without platform-specific code paths.\n\n**Single executable distribution.** Anvil is a CLI tool. Users should install a single executable file — no runtime, no interpreter, n Source: ADR-011-programming-language-selection.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:Accepted.","decision":"Anvil will be implemented using Go.\n\nThis decision is based on the product and architectural requirements established across PRD-001 and ADR-001 through ADR-010.\n\nGo is an implementation choice. It does not influence the architectural boundaries, domain definitions, lifecycle model, or any other architectural concept defined in previous documents.\n\nThe architecture remains language-independent. Future ADRs may specify implementation details within Go, but the architectural model — domains, lifecycle, state, pipeline, process runner, adapters, CLI — is defined independently of the implementation language and would remain valid if a different language were chosen.","consequences":"### 9.1 Positive Consequences\n\n**Single binary distribution.** Users install a single file. There is no runtime dependency, no PATH manipulation beyond placing the binary, and no version compatibility checks.\n\n**Fast, consistent startup.** Every command starts in milliseconds. The first invocation is as fast as subsequent invocations.\n\n**Cross-platform support from day one.** Linux 